\subsection{Decision Variables}
\label{sec:dvars}

\paragraph{Decision variables.}
The program's decision is, at every intersection $i$ and every integer step
$t$, \emph{which signal phase is green}.  That is the only quantity a
controller sets directly: everything else below is either a bookkeeping device
for that one question or a state the objective reads, and each is fixed by the
equations cited once $x$ is chosen.

\begin{longtable}{lp{3.5cm}p{7cm}}
\hline
Symbol & Domain & Definition \\
\hline
\endhead
\hline
\endfoot
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textbf{The control (set directly)}} \\*
$x_{i,p,t}$ & $\{0,1\}$ & 1 if phase $p$ at $i$ is \textbf{green} during integer step $t$ \\
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textbf{Flow state (integer; propagated by \cref{eq:mv_q,eq:mv_dcap})}} \\*
$Q_{i,m,v,t}$ & $\Z_{\ge 0}$ & Predicted queue (veh) on movement $m$, class $v$ at $(i,t)$ \\
$d_{i,m,v,t}$ & $\Z_{\ge 0}$ & Predicted discharge (veh) on movement $m$, class $v$ at $(i,t)$ \\
$Q^w_{i,m,v,t}$ & $\Z_{\ge 0}$ & Queued waiting vehicles on movement $m$, class $v$ at $(i,t)$ \\
$D_{i,s,v,t}$ & $\Z_{\ge 0}$ & Delay (veh\,s) on section $s$, class $v$ at $(i,t)$; enters $Z_1$ weighted by $\rho_v$ \\
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textbf{Progression and bus clocks (integer; fixed by $x$)}} \\*
$h_{ij,t}$ & $\{0,1\}$ & Lagged main-green overlap, pinned by \cref{eq:band_min} to $\min(x_{i,p_b^i,t},\,x_{j,p_b^j,t+\ell_{ij}})$: an auxiliary flag, not a free choice \\
$e_{b,i,t}$ & $\{0,1\}$ & 1 if bus $b$ passes intersection $i$ at step $t$; pinned to $t=\tau_{b,i}$ by \cref{eq:arr_assign,eq:arr_link} \\
$\tau_{b,i}$ & $\Z_{\ge 0}$ & Passage step of bus $b$ at intersection $i$ \\
$\sigma_{b,i}^+,\sigma_{b,i}^-$ & $\Z_{\ge 0}$ & Late/early parts of the schedule deviation \\
$s^3_{b,i}$ & $\Z_{\ge 0}$ & Lateness slack (steps) for the exact-penalty cap \\
\end{longtable}

None of the last two blocks carries a degree of freedom: given the phase
trajectory, $h$ is the minimum of two green flags, $e$ and $\tau$ follow from
the bus recursion (\cref{eq:arr_prop}), and the flow state is propagated by
conservation (\cref{eq:mv_q}).  This is what it means to say the program has
one decision.

Read with respect to the main (bus-priority) movement, the decision variable has
a corridor-level name. Let $p_b^i \in \Psp_i$ be the phase that serves the main
through movement at intersection $i$, and let $W_{i,c}$ be the set of seconds
in cycle window $c$; both are defined in the notation table above. Define
\begin{equation}
  x^{\text{main}}_{i,t} \;\triangleq\; x_{i,p_b^i,t},
  \qquad
  G^{\text{main}}_{i,c} \;\triangleq\; \sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x^{\text{main}}_{i,t},
  \label{eq:x_main_reading}
\end{equation}
so $x^{\text{main}}_{i,t}=1$ exactly when the main phase is green at $i$ in
second $t$, and $G^{\text{main}}_{i,c}$ is the main-phase green time in window
$c$ (in seconds, since one step is one second). Equation
\eqref{eq:x_main_reading} is a definition, not a constraint: it names the
main-phase component of the decision and its per-cycle green total so that the
objective \cref{eq:Z1} and the green/cycle bounds
\cref{eq:g_bounds,eq:c_bounds} can be written in corridor terms. Delay is then
attributed by approach class $\sigma(\cdot)$, giving $D_{i,\text{main},v,t}$ and
$D_{i,\text{side},v,t}$ from the one flag, and lane capacity enters only
through saturation.

\subsection{Timing Outcomes: Cycle, Green, and Offsets}
\label{sec:stage_a}

There is no separate design program and no fixed-time plan standing before
the optimiser.  The cycle, the per-phase green, and the offset are
\emph{reported outcomes} of the committed signal plan, not decisions of a
prior stage: the program chooses the green/red pattern $x_{i,p,t}$ and the
quantities below are read off it.  What the timing must satisfy is stated
once, as bounds on the green-step counts, by \cref{eq:g_bounds,eq:c_bounds}.
A conventional fixed-time plan is the \emph{special case} in which the
program takes no action and the bounds pin the timing to a single point---a
warm start, not a prior stage.

\paragraph{Timing inputs.} For each intersection $i$ and movement
$m \in \Msp_i$, the exogenous arrival flow $n_{i,m,v,t}$ (veh/step, integer),
and for each phase its saturation $\mu_{i,p}$ (\cref{eq:mu_lanes}); for the
bus phase $p_b^i$ the timetable intensity $\vartheta_i$ (buses per cycle)
and the occupancy $\rho_{\text{bus}}$.  These size the bounds.  They are
data: the arrival \emph{count} is absorbed by the queue recursion
(\cref{eq:mv_q}), never by re-optimising the timing against the mean
(\cref{sec:stochastic}).

\paragraph{Cycle and its bounds.} The nominal cycle $C_i$ is a parameter
fixed for the junction, with bounds $C_i^{\min}, C_i^{\max}$ (integer).  The
program may realise any green-step total within
$[C_i^{\min}-L_i,\, C_i^{\max}-L_i]$ per cycle window; a starting value,
used only to initialise the clock and as a warm start, is the Webster value
on the \emph{critical} flow ratio, computed offline and supplied as data
($C_i^{0}$, clamped to the bounds).  When the critical flow ratio is at or
above unity the junction is oversaturated and the program falls back on the
measured queue state.  Integer arithmetic throughout, so the cycle is a whole
number of seconds.

\paragraph{Green splits.} Green is apportioned by the program through the
open indicators $x_{i,p,t}$ subject to \cref{eq:g_bounds,eq:c_bounds}; the
split is the count of green steps per phase per window, not a separate
decision.  A conventional Webster split is the warm start
$\bar{x}_{i,p,t}$.

\paragraph{Offsets.} The offset of intersection $i$ in cycle $c$ is defined
as the first integer step $t$ in $W_{i,c}$ with $x_{i,p_b^i,t}=1$ (the
start of the bus-phase green); it is a \emph{reported outcome} of the phase
schedule.  Coordination is enforced directly by the lagged overlap
constraints of \cref{eq:band_min}, not by an offset variable, which removes
the modulo arithmetic and the multiple offset models that previously
coexisted.  A starting value follows from progression---the timetable fixes a
desired running time $\Theta^{\text{ff}}_{ij}$ between consecutive
intersections---but the running value is not fixed.

\paragraph{Lane capacity.} Saturation is additive over the lanes serving a
phase:
\begin{equation}
  \mu_{i,p} \;=\; \sum_{m:\,\Psp_i(m) = p} \;\sum_{\ell \in \Lsp_{i,m}} \mu_{i,\ell},
  \qquad
  \mu_{i,m} \;=\; \sum_{\ell \in \Lsp_{i,m}} \mu_{i,\ell},
  \label{eq:mu_lanes}
\end{equation}
so a phase inherits the capacity of its lanes and the demand of its
movements, and no timing constraint is ever written per lane.

\subsection{Objective Function}
\label{sec:objectives}

The program has exactly one objective: it minimises total passenger delay.
Every other quantity is a cap or a monitor, never a second objective.  All
clocks are integer ($\Delta t = 1$); the delay quantities below are built from
the veh\,s variables $D_{i,s,v,t}$, converted to passenger-seconds by the
occupancies $\rho_{\text{bus}} = 40$, $\rho_{\text{car}} = 1.2$.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\caption{The objective outline. Exactly one term ($Z_1$) is minimised; $Z_2$ is
an optional linear term at a fixed coefficient; $Z_3$ is an exact-penalty cap;
$Z_4$ is a post-run reported metric, not a constraint of this program.}
\label{tab:objective_terms}
\small
\begin{tabular}{p{2.9cm}p{3.3cm}p{3.6cm}p{3.4cm}}
\toprule
Term & Exact form & What it measures & Role in the criterion \\
\midrule
$Z_1$ total passenger delay (pax\,s) & \cref{eq:Z1} &
Signal-caused queueing delay, occupancy-weighted & \textbf{Minimised} (the one objective) \\
$Z_2$ green-wave progression & \cref{eq:Z2} &
Lagged main-green overlap & Optional linear term at fixed coefficient \\
$Z_3$ aggregate lateness (steps$\times$bus) & \cref{eq:Z3} &
Timetable violation of every bus & Exact-penalty cap \\
$Z_4$ corridor travel time (veh\,h) & \cref{eq:Z4} &
End-to-end bus and car travel time & Reported metric, not constrained here \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Minimised: total person delay ($Z_1$).}
Person delay is the single currency throughout: occupancy times vehicle
delay.  With $\rho_v$ the occupancy (pax/veh) of class $v$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:person_delay}
  P_{i,t} = \sum_{s \in \Ssp} \sum_{v \in \Vsp} \rho_v\, D_{i,s,v,t}
  \qquad \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp,
\end{equation}
in person-seconds.  $Z_1$ is person delay with an extra section weight $w_s$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Z1}
  Z_1 = \sum_{t \in \Tsp} \sum_{i \in \Isp}
    \Bigl( w_{\text{main}}\,\rho_{\text{bus}}\, D_{i,\text{main},\text{bus},t}
    + w_{\text{main}}\,\rho_{\text{car}}\, D_{i,\text{main},\text{car},t}
    + w_{\text{side}}\,\rho_{\text{car}}\, D_{i,\text{side},\text{car},t} \Bigr).
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Progression term ($Z_2$).}
With lagged overlap $h_{ij,t}$ and main-platoon flow $q_{ij,t}$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Z2}
  Z_2 = \sum_{t \in \Tsp} \sum_{(i,j) \in \Esp}
    q_{ij,t}\, h_{ij,t}.
\end{equation}
Perfect overlap scores $q$ per step; the progression tolerance is enforced
by the minimum overlap $B^{\min}_{ij}$ of \cref{eq:band_min}.  By default
$Z_2$ is monitored and not optimised; if the modeller chooses to price
progression it enters the one objective as $-\omega_2 Z_2$ at a fixed
coefficient $\omega_2$---overlap is rewarded, hence the minus sign
(\cref{eq:opt_crit,tab:objective_terms}).

\paragraph{Capped: schedule adherence ($Z_3$).}
Lateness is $\sigma_{b,i}^+ = \max(0, \tau_{b,i} - \bar{\tau}_{b,i})$
(\cref{eq:sched_split}); earliness is free:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Z3}
  Z_3 = \sum_{b \in \Bsp} \sum_{i \in \Rcal_b} \omega_{b}\, \sigma_{b,i}^+,
  \qquad \omega_b \ge 1.
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Reported: corridor travel time ($Z_4$).}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Z4}
  Z_4 = TT^{\text{bus}} + TT^{\text{car}}, \qquad
  TT^{v} = \sum_{n \in \mathcal{N}^v} \bigl(t_n^{\text{exit}} - t_n^{\text{entry}}\bigr),
  \quad v \in \{\text{bus},\text{car}\}.
\end{equation}
$Z_4$ is a post-run veh\,h outcome (network bus and car total-time
statistics), never a per-step variable and not a constraint of this program.
The throughput cap is enforced in the online selection layer, not here.

\subsubsection{Optimality Criterion}
\label{sec:objective_crit}
The program carries one minimised scalar. $\omega_2 \ge 0$ is a fixed
coefficient set before the solve---$\omega_2 = 0$ is the delay-only default
and any $\omega_2 > 0$ simply adds the $Z_2$ term of
\cref{tab:objective_terms}---so it is data, not a mode and not a tuned online
weight:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:opt_crit}
  \min_{x,Q,d,Q^w,D,e,\tau,\sigma,h,s^3}\;
  Z_1 + \lambda_3 \sum_{b \in \Bsp}\sum_{i \in \Rcal_b} s^3_{b,i}
  - \omega_2 Z_2
  \quad \text{s.t.} \quad
  \text{constraints \cref{eq:gate_excl,eq:sched_cap}},\;
  \sigma^+_{b,i} \le \epsilon^{\text{late}} + s^3_{b,i}\;\; \forall b,i.
\end{equation}
The lateness cap is enforced through the slack $s^3$ priced at
$\lambda_3$: when $\lambda_3$ exceeds the largest possible delay saving
attributable to one late second, a unit of lateness is never traded for less
than a unit of delay, so the cap is exact whenever it is feasible and the
program falls back to minimising lateness only when the cap is infeasible.
The all--no-action schedule always satisfies physical feasibility and the
slacks, so the program degrades gracefully to fixed time under saturation.

\subsection{Constraints}
\label{sec:milp_constraints}

\paragraph{Index conventions.} Throughout \cref{sec:milp}: $i,j \in \Isp$
intersections; $t \in \Tsp$ integer steps; $p \in \Psp_i$ phases
($p_b^i$ bus phase); $m \in \Msp_i$ movements; $v \in \Vsp$ classes;
$b \in \Bsp$ buses with route $\Rcal_b$; $s \in \Ssp$ sections;
$(i,j) \in \Esp$ links; $c$ cycle windows.  Every constraint below is
written in canonical form $a^\top z \le b$ or $a^\top z = b$: lower bounds
appear with negated coefficients.  No $\min$, $\max$, $\mathrm{abs}$,
$\bmod$, or ratio appears in any constraint; products of a binary and a
bounded variable are avoided by construction.

\paragraph{Signal-switching physics.}
\begin{align}
  \sum_{p \in \Psp_i} x_{i,p,t} &\le 1
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:gate_excl} \\[3pt]
  G_i^{\min}\,(x_{i,p,t} - x_{i,p,t-1}) - \sum_{r=t}^{\min(t+G_i^{\min}-1,T)} x_{i,p,r} &\le 0
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:gate_dwell} \\[3pt]
  x_{i,p,t} + x_{i,p',t+r} &\le 1
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; (p,p') \in \mathcal{F}_i,\; r=1,\dots,I_{\text{ig}},\; t \le T-r \label{eq:gate_clear}
\end{align}

\paragraph{Green and cycle bounds.}
\begin{align}
  -\sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t} &\le -G_i^{\min}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; c \label{eq:g_bounds} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t} &\le G_i^{\max}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; c \\[3pt]
  -\sum_{p \in \Psp_i}\sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t} &\le -(C_i^{\min}-L_i)
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; c \label{eq:c_bounds} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{p \in \Psp_i}\sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t} &\le C_i^{\max}-L_i
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; c
\end{align}

\paragraph{Flow and storage.}
\begin{align}
  Q_{i,m,v,t+1} - Q_{i,m,v,t} - A_{i,m,v,t} + d_{i,m,v,t} &= 0
    && \forall i,m,v,\; t < T \label{eq:mv_q} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{v \in \Vsp} d_{i,m,v,t} - \mu_{i,m}\, x_{i,p(m),t} &\le 0
    && \forall i,m,t \label{eq:mv_dcap} \\[3pt]
  d_{i,m,v,t} - Q_{i,m,v,t} - A_{i,m,v,t} &\le 0
    && \forall i,m,v,t \\[3pt]
  Q^w_{i,m,v,t} - Q_{i,m,v,t} &\le 0 \label{eq:mv_wait} \\[3pt]
  Q^w_{i,m,v,t} + K_{i,m}\, x_{i,p(m),t} &\le K_{i,m} \\[3pt]
  Q_{i,m,v,t} - Q^w_{i,m,v,t} - K_{i,m}\, x_{i,p(m),t} &\le 0 \\[3pt]
  \sum_{m \in \Msp_i(s,v)} Q^w_{i,m,v,t} - D_{i,s,v,t} &\le 0 \label{eq:mv_waitsum} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{v \in \Vsp} Q_{i,m,v,t} - K_{i,m} &\le 0 \label{eq:storage_cap} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{v \in \Vsp} d_{i,m,v,t} + \sum_{v \in \Vsp} Q_{j,m^+(m),v,t} - K_{j,m^+(m)} + M^{\text{big}}\,(1 - x_{i,p(m),t}) &\le M^{\text{big}}
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; m,\; t \label{eq:discharge_space}
\end{align}
where $A_{i,m,v,t}$ is the realised arrival count (endogenous through
\cref{eq:cons_phase}) and $p(m)$ is the phase serving movement $m$.

\paragraph{Network conservation.}
\begin{align}
  N^{\text{in}}_{ij,v,t} - n_{ij,v,t} - \sum_{m \in \Msp_i:\, m \to (i,j)} d_{i,m,v,t-\tau_{ij}} &= 0
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; v,\; t \label{eq:cons_link} \\[3pt]
  A_{j,m,v,t} - \sum_{i:\,(i,j)\in\Esp} \gamma^m_{ij}\, N^{\text{in}}_{ij,v,t} - n^m_{j,v,t} &= 0
    && \forall j,m,v,t \label{eq:cons_phase}
\end{align}
where $\gamma^m_{ij} \in \{0,1\}$ is the fixed geometry mapping link $(i,j)$
to movement $m$ at $j$, and terms with $t-\tau_{ij}<0$ are zero.

\paragraph{Corridor coordination (lagged progression).}
\begin{align}
  h_{ij,t} - x_{i,p_b^i,t} &\le 0 \label{eq:band_min} \\[3pt]
  h_{ij,t} - x_{j,p_b^j,t+\ell_{ij}} &\le 0 \\[3pt]
  x_{i,p_b^i,t} + x_{j,p_b^j,t+\ell_{ij}} - 1 - h_{ij,t} &\le 0 \\[3pt]
  -\sum_{t \in \Tsp} h_{ij,t} &\le -B^{\min}_{ij}
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp
\end{align}
with $x_{j,p,t+\ell} := 0$ when $t+\ell > T$.

\paragraph{Bus clocks and schedule adherence.}
\begin{align}
  \sum_{t \in \Tsp} e_{b,i,t} - 1 &= 0
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:arr_assign} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{t \in \Tsp} t\, e_{b,i,t} - \tau_{b,i} &= 0
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:arr_link} \\[3pt]
  e_{b,i,t} - x_{i,p_b^i,t} &\le 0
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:prog_bus} \\[3pt]
  \tau_{b,i_h} + \tau^{\text{ff}}_{i_h,i_{h+1}} - \tau_{b,i_{h+1}} &\le 0
    && \forall b,\; h=0,\dots,N-1 \label{eq:arr_prop} \\[3pt]
  \tau_b^{\text{detect}} - \tau_{b,i_0} &\le 0
    && \forall b \in \Bsp \label{eq:arr_init} \\[3pt]
  \tau_{b,i} - \bar{\tau}_{b,i} - \sigma^+_{b,i} + \sigma^-_{b,i} &= 0
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:sched_split} \\[3pt]
  \sigma^+_{b,i} - s^3_{b,i} &\le \epsilon^{\text{late}}
    && \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b \label{eq:sched_cap}
\end{align}

\paragraph{Domains.}
\begin{align*}
  & x_{i,p,t} \in \{0,1\},\quad h_{ij,t} \in \{0,1\},\quad e_{b,i,t} \in \{0,1\}, \\
  & Q_{i,m,v,t}, d_{i,m,v,t}, Q^w_{i,m,v,t}, D_{i,s,v,t}, s^3_{b,i} \in \Z_{\ge 0}, \\
  & \tau_{b,i}, \sigma^\pm_{b,i} \in \Z_{\ge 0}.
\end{align*}

\paragraph{Re-optimisation.} \label{sec:offset_recourse} The exact program is
clairvoyant, so it is solved once over a horizon rather than re-solved online.
Its receding-horizon counterpart is the same program over a window
$\Tsp(t_c)=\{t_c,\dots,t_c+T_w\}$ with the initial state replaced by the
measured state $\hat{X}_{t_c}$:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{P}\bigl(\hat{X}_{t_c},\,\Tsp(t_c)\bigr)
  \;=\;
  \mathcal{P}\big|_{\,\Tsp(t_c),\; X_{t_c}=\hat{X}_{t_c}} .
  \label{eq:reopt}
\end{equation}
Only the initial condition changes, so feasibility carries over from the
warm-start plan, which satisfies every constraint of the shorter window.
Offsets are read off the resulting plan in the reported quantities above and
compared with the previous plan; their difference is the shift an
offset-correction action \texttt{OC} would apply, and the online controller of
\cref{sec:solutions} decides whether that shift is worth taking.

\paragraph{Reported (non-constraint) quantities.} Realised green is the open
indicator itself,
\begin{equation}
  g_{i,p,t} = x_{i,p,t} \qquad \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; t \in \Tsp
  \label{eq:gate_green}
\end{equation}
and the following are read off the committed schedule and are not variables of
the program:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Offset} $\phi_{i,c}$: the first step $t \in W_{i,c}$ with
        $x_{i,p_b^i,t}=1$.
  \item \textbf{Realised green} $g_{i,p,t} = x_{i,p,t}$, so $g=x$ (\cref{eq:gate_green}).
  \item \textbf{Green split} $G_{i,p,c} = \sum_{t \in W_{i,c}} x_{i,p,t}$.
  \item \textbf{Cycle} $C_{i,c} = \sum_{p \in \Psp_i} G_{i,p,c} + L_i$.
  \item \textbf{Action label} at $(i,t)$: the letter ($\texttt{GE}$,
        $\texttt{INS}$, $\texttt{ER}$, $\texttt{GR}$, $\texttt{VP}$,
        $\texttt{PT}$, $\texttt{OC}$, $\texttt{NA}$) whose phase perturbation
        best matches the change in $x_{i,\cdot,t}$ relative to the warm start
        $\bar{x}_{i,\cdot,t}$; the online controller of
        \cref{sec:solutions} chooses the letter directly.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Equivalent Markovian Formulation}
\label{sec:markovian}

This subsection recasts the program defined in this section as a finite-horizon dynamic program over an estimated state.

\paragraph{State: the model's memory.}
A state is sufficient only if it carries every quantity that a later
constraint can read. The reduced tuple $(x_{t-1},Q_t,\tau_t)$ used in an
earlier draft is \emph{not} sufficient: minimum green and intergreen are
window constraints, the green/cycle bounds are per-cycle counts, the
progression overlap reads a progression lag, and buses upstream of a junction are
not in $\tau_t$. The sufficient state is
\begin{equation}
  X_t=\bigl(x_{t-1},\ \kappa_t,\ \zeta_t,\ W_t,\ Q_t,\ N_t,\ T_t\bigr),
  \label{eq:markov_state}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item $x_{t-1}=\{x_{i,p,t-1}\}$ is the previous phase-state vector;
  \item $\kappa_t$ holds the remaining minimum-green and intergreen counters
        per $(i,p)$ and $(i,p,p')$ (non-negative integers bounded by
        $G_i^{\min}$ and $I_{\text{ig}}$);
  \item $\zeta_t$ holds the current cycle-window index and the green-step
        counts per $(i,p,c)$ required by \cref{eq:g_bounds,eq:c_bounds};
  \item $W_t=(x_{t-1},\dots,x_{t-\ell_{\max}})$ is the history of phase states over the
        longest progression lag
        $\ell_{\max}=\max_{(i,j)\in\Esp}\ell_{ij}$;
  \item $Q_t=\{Q_{i,m,v,t}\}$ collects the movement queues, including the
        downstream queues read by the storage and discharge constraints;
  \item $N_t=\{N^{\text{in}}_{ij,v,t}\}$ is the in-transit pipeline on every
        link, i.e.\ the vehicles already discharged upstream but not yet
        arrived downstream; it carries the free-flow lag $\tau_{ij}$ of the
        network conservation \cref{eq:cons_link};
  \item $T_t$ collects the clocks and positions of \emph{all} buses that
        have entered the network, not only those past a junction.
\end{itemize}
Every entry is integer or a finite binary vector, so the state is finite.

\paragraph{Transition and stage cost.}
The control is
$u_t=\{x_{i,p,t}\}_{i,p}\in\{0,1\}^{\sum_{i\in\Isp}|\Psp_i|}$. To make the flow a
function of the state and control, impose the model's \emph{service closure}:
\begin{equation}
  (S)\qquad
  d_{i,m,v,t}=\text{the maximal feasible discharge at saturation }
  \mu_{i,m}x_{i,p(m),t}
  \text{ in a fixed class order,}
  \label{eq:service_closure}
\end{equation}
subject to available vehicles and downstream storage. Under $(S)$ the
discharge is not a free decision; it is the rate at which the model serves a
green movement. Then
\begin{equation}
  X_{t+1}=F(X_t,u_t)
  \label{eq:markov_trans}
\end{equation}
is deterministic: $Q$ follows \cref{eq:mv_q}, $d$ follows $(S)$, $Q^w$
follows \cref{eq:mv_wait} ($Q^w=Q$ on red, $0$ on green), the counters
follow their definitions, and the bus clocks follow \cref{eq:arr_prop} with
the \emph{first-green passage rule} (a bus crosses at the first step at
which it is at the stop line and its phase is green).

The stage cost is the delay of step $t$:
\begin{equation}
  c_t(X,u)=\sum_{i\in\Isp}\sum_{s\in\Ssp}\sum_{v\in\Vsp}
    w_s\,\rho_v\sum_{m\in\Msp_i(s,v)} Q^w_{i,m,v,t}(X,u),
  \label{eq:stage_cost}
\end{equation}
which is $Z_1$ of \cref{eq:Z1} term by term once $D$ is at its lower bound
\cref{eq:mv_waitsum}; the bound is tight at any optimum because
$w_s,\rho_v>0$.

\begin{theorem}[Bellman equivalence for the delay-only program]
\label{thm:milp_bellman}
Assume the service closure $(S)$, a fixed initial state $X_0$, and the
delay-only criterion $Z_1$ (no progression term and no lateness penalty).
Let $\mathbb{F}$ be the set of phase-state sequences $(u_0,\dots,u_T)$ satisfying
the constraints in
\cref{eq:gate_excl,eq:gate_dwell,eq:gate_clear,eq:g_bounds,eq:c_bounds,eq:mv_q,eq:mv_dcap,eq:mv_wait,eq:mv_waitsum,eq:storage_cap,eq:discharge_space,eq:cons_link,eq:cons_phase,eq:arr_assign,eq:arr_link,eq:prog_bus,eq:arr_prop,eq:arr_init,eq:sched_split,eq:sched_cap}.
Then the delay-only MILP
\[
  Z^*=\min_{(u_0,\dots,u_T)\in\mathbb{F}}\;\sum_{t=0}^{T}c_t(X_t,u_t),
  \qquad X_{t+1}=F(X_t,u_t),
\]
and the finite-horizon dynamic program
\[
  V_t(X)=\min_{u\in\Ucal(X)}\bigl[c_t(X,u)+V_{t+1}(F(X,u))\bigr],
  \qquad V_{T+1}\equiv 0,
\]
have the same optimal value, $Z^*=V_0(X_0)$, and the same set of optimal
phase-state sequences.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $t$, from $T$ down to $0$. Let $\mathbb{F}_t(X)$ be the set of
remaining phase-state sequences $(u_t,\dots,u_T)$ feasible from state $X$ at step $t$, and let
$J_t(X)=\min_{\mathbb{F}_t(X)}\sum_{k=t}^{T}c_k$.
Base: $\mathbb{F}_{T+1}(X)=\{\emptyset\}$ and $J_{T+1}=0=V_{T+1}$.
Step: for any $u\in\Ucal(X)$, a tail is feasible from $F(X,u)$ if and only if
the concatenated tail is feasible from $X$, because every model constraint
under the state \cref{eq:markov_state} is either a constraint on $u$ alone or
on $(X,u)$: the counters $\kappa_t$ enforce minimum green and intergreen,
$\zeta_t$ enforces the per-cycle counts, $W_t$ carries the progression lag,
$N_t$ carries the free-flow pipeline, and $Q_t$ carries the downstream
storage. Hence
\[
  J_t(X)=\min_{u\in\Ucal(X)}\bigl[c_t(X,u)+J_{t+1}(F(X,u))\bigr].
\]
By the induction hypothesis $J_{t+1}=V_{t+1}$, so $J_t=V_t$. At $t=0$,
$J_0(X_0)$ is the MILP optimum and $V_0(X_0)$ is the DP optimum; the argmin
sets coincide at every step, so the optimal phase-state sequences coincide too.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Deterministic equivalent program]
The delay-only MILP is the deterministic equivalent program of the dynamic
program above.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:milp_bellman} the feasible phase-state sequences and the
feasible policies coincide, and the two objectives coincide term by term.
Hence the two problems have the same feasible set and the same objective,
so their optima coincide.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{The full criterion.}
The scored program also carries the progression term $-\omega_2 Z_2$ and the
lateness penalty $\lambda_3\sum s^3$ of \cref{eq:opt_crit}. Both are
stage-additive under the same state: $h_{ij,t-\ell_{ij}}$ is read from $W_t$
and $u_t$, the overlap count is one of the counters in $\zeta_t$ (with the
terminal requirement $\sum_t h_{ij,t}\ge B^{\min}_{ij}$), and
$\sigma^+_{b,i}$ is read from $T_t$ when bus $b$ passes $i$. Adding those
terms to $c_t$ and imposing the terminal count constraint leaves the
induction of Theorem~\ref{thm:milp_bellman} unchanged, so the full program is
also the DEP of a dynamic program---but of the \emph{augmented} state
\cref{eq:markov_state}, not of the reduced tuple
$(x_{t-1},Q_t,\tau_t)$. The reduced tuple omits the min-green/intergreen
counters, the cycle counts, the progression lag, and the upstream buses; with
it the Markov property fails. The deployed controller does not use the
reduced tuple as a DP state; it uses the measured state and the guards of
\cref{sec:online_guards}. The exact program is reported as a time-expanded
MILP and is never solved as a DP.

\subsubsection{Auxiliary-variable lemma: what the service closure fixes}
\label{sec:holding}
\begin{lemma}[Auxiliary variables]\label{lem:aux}
Under the service closure $(S)$ and a phase sequence, the state trajectory
$X_t$ is uniquely determined by $X_0$ and the phase sequence, and the
auxiliaries $(D,h,e,\tau,\sigma^\pm,s^3)$ are determined at an optimum.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$Q_{t+1}$ follows \cref{eq:mv_q}; $d$ follows $(S)$; $Q^w$ follows
\cref{eq:mv_wait}; $D$ follows \cref{eq:mv_waitsum}, and at an optimum the
bound is tight because $w_s,\rho_v>0$; $h$ follows from the history of phase states;
$e,\tau$ follow the first-green passage rule; and $\sigma^\pm,s^3$ follow
\cref{eq:sched_split,eq:sched_cap}. Two qualifications matter. First, in the
raw inequality system $d$ is a free integer variable; $(S)$ is exactly what
turns it into a function, so the lemma is false without $(S)$. Second, $D$
is tight only at an optimum; away from the optimum \cref{eq:mv_waitsum} can
be slack, which does not affect the optimum because the objective is
increasing in $D$. With those two points, $F$ is well defined and no extra
history variable is needed beyond \cref{eq:markov_state}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Arrival Uncertainty}
\label{sec:stochastic}

\paragraph{Basis for the uncertainty treatment.}
The timing bounds of Sec.~\ref{sec:stage_a} are sized for the mean demand, so
the plan they admit is a mean plan.  Counting is a discrete random process,
so even at the mean rate the per-step count on a movement fluctuates, and it
is that fluctuation, not the mean, that decides whether a queue forms behind
a red.  We therefore treat the arrival \emph{count} as uncertain, and state
which part of the program carries the consequence.  This is deliberately
\emph{not} a two-stage stochastic program: the program is never solved here
(\cref{sec:milp_status}), so a sample-average apparatus would claim a
distributional guarantee we do not establish.

\paragraph{The uncertain count.}
Let $\bar{A}_{i,m,v}$ be the mean flow on movement $m$, class $v$ at $i$ and
$\bar{A}_{i,m,v,t}$ its per-step mean. The realised count is
\begin{equation}
  A_{i,m,v,t} \;=\; \Bigl[\, \bar{A}_{i,m,v,t} + \xi_{i,m,v,t} \,\Bigr]^{\!\!+},
  \qquad \xi_{i,m,v,t} \sim \mathcal{N}\!\bigl(0,\ \bar{A}_{i,m,v,t} + \kappa\bigr),
  \label{eq:arr_process}
\end{equation}
where $[\,\cdot\,]^{+}$ keeps the count non-negative.  Bus entries carry
their own independent uncertainty,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:arr_error}
  \tau_{b,i_0} = \hat{\tau}_{b,i_0} + \epsilon_b,
  \qquad \epsilon_b \text{ Gaussian, mean zero, variance } \varsigma^2.
\end{equation}

