\subsection{Computational Status}
\label{sec:milp_status}

The word ``exact'' refers to the formulation, not to a solved benchmark. No
MILP objective value, optimality gap, solution time, node count, or
branch-and-bound statistic is reported, because the full-horizon program was
not solved to optimality on the Kelvin Grove corridor in this study, and no
number from it is used to rank a controller. All percentage changes in the
Abstract and in \cref{sec:results} are microsimulation outcomes (Aimsun Next,
\texttt{champion\_kg.csv}) measured against the \texttt{NO\_TSP} fixed-time
baseline, not against an MILP optimum.

\paragraph{Computational scale.}
At $\Delta t = 1$\,s the scored horizon is $T = 4500$ steps (1.25\,h) over
$|\Isp| = 12$ intersections with $|\Psp_i| \approx 4$ phases each. The
time-expanded model therefore has approximately
$|\Isp|\,|\Psp_i|\,T \approx 2.2\times10^5$ binary phase-state variables
$x_{i,p,t}$, of order $10^5$ integer queue, discharge, delay,
progression-overlap, and bus-clock variables, and of order $10^6$ constraints.
These are order-of-magnitude counts from the instance dimensions, not solver
statistics. A branch-and-bound solve at this scale is not compatible with the
one-second decision cadence of the controller, nor with the compute budget of
this study, and no attempt is made here to obtain an optimality certificate.
The program is therefore used as a reference formulation, not as an online
solver.

\paragraph{Role of the formulation.}
The program is the normative definition of the control problem---what should be
done at every intersection and second under perfect prediction---and the
reference against which the online methods are evaluated. The equivalent
Bellman formulation in \cref{sec:markovian} is a representation of the same
program, not a second model; the online methods in \cref{sec:solutions}
approximate it event by event. Its value is definitional and comparative rather
than numerical: one model, one criterion, and one set of constraints against
which the six evaluated methods are compared on the common microsimulation
criterion.

\subsection{Model Boundary}
\label{sec:milp_boundary}

The program is an \emph{exact} MILP of a \emph{simplified} model: a
point-queue network with finite link storage, integer clocks, and a fixed
cycle envelope.  It is not an exact optimisation of the LWR kinematic-wave,
shockwave, or cell-transmission dynamics described in \cref{sec:dynamics};
those are background \emph{estimation} models used by the online layer
(\cref{sec:solutions}) to predict queues and ETAs, and they enter this
program only through the exogenous arrivals $n_{i,m,v,t}$ and the storage
bounds $K_{i,m}$.  Likewise, the program does not model moving bottlenecks
or spillback beyond the finite-storage block (\cref{eq:storage_cap,eq:discharge_space}).
Stating the boundary precisely is what makes the word ``exact'' honest: the
program is exact with respect to the model it defines, and the model is
deliberately simpler than the traffic it approximates.

This completes the mathematical program. The online methods that approximate it are described in Sec.~\ref{sec:solutions}.
