\centering
\caption{Batch experiment design: what was done in each phase.  Every cell is
a distinct random-seed replication of the full 1.25\,h corridor simulation at
the stated demand; learners pre-train on four held-out seeds (800--1100) and
are frozen before scoring, so no arm is scored on a seed it trained on.}
\label{tab:batch_design}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{p{2.6cm}p{3.4cm}p{5.2cm}r}
\hline
Phase (question) & What varies & What is fixed & Runs \\
\hline
1. Strategy comparison (which controller wins at nominal demand?) & 6 strategy arms (M0--M5, \cref{tab:methods}) $\times$ 5 eval seeds (300--700) $\times$ demand $\times$1.0 & Geometry, demand profile, measured state feed, guard protocol & 30 \\
2. Bus-demand sensitivity (does the winner survive bus-service change?) & Phase-1 champion + fixed-time baseline $\times$ 5 bus-service levels $\times$ 5 seeds & Strategy set, car demand, geometry & 50 \\
3. Demand and intervention sweep (does the winner survive demand change?) & Champion $\times$ 6 car-demand scalars (0.6--1.6) $\times$ 5 seeds $\times$ 3 action-family tactics (timing: GE$+$GR; swaps: INS$+$early-red; both) $\times$ 3 intervention spacings (default / 40\,s / 120\,s) + matched baseline & Geometry, bus service & 300 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Phase~1 -- method ladder (adaptive base $+$ priority layer).}
Five methods are evaluated over \emph{five} random-seed replications
(seeds 300, 400, 500, 600, 700) at demand scalar~1.0; each replication is the
full 1.25\,h corridor simulation. The five seeds are the apples-to-apples
scoring set for \emph{every} method. The learning heads (M2, M3) additionally
pre-train on four held-out seeds (800--1100) and are then frozen before
scoring, so no method is scored on a seed it trained on. All methods decide on
the same \emph{measured} state feed -- verified section-total flow and a
measured queue floor -- rather than an analytic warm-up estimate. The ladder is:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
  \item M0 \texttt{NO\_TSP} -- fixed-time reference.
  \item M1 \texttt{CHAMP\_ADAPTIVE\_AIMSUN\_DSW} -- adaptive plan, no bus priority.
  \item M2 \texttt{CHAMP\_ADAPTIVE\_DSW\_MARL\_BUS} -- learned bus-weighted
        priority (CPD-QL head).
  \item M3 \texttt{CHAMP\_ADAPTIVE\_DSW\_CELLQ\_GATED} -- admission-controlled
        CellQLearn priority (net-benefit test $+$ cascade).
  \item M4 \texttt{CHAMP\_ADAPTIVE\_DSW\_GAME\_BUS} -- Nash/GAME bus priority.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Phase~2 -- bus-demand sensitivity.}
The Phase-1 champion and the fixed-time baseline are swept over five
bus-service levels (timetable frequency multipliers 0.5--3.0) at fixed car
demand, five seeds each: this isolates whether the win depends on how many
buses are on the road.

\textbf{Phase~3 -- demand and intervention sweep.}
The champion is swept over the car-demand, tactic, and spacing factorial of
\cref{tab:batch_design} with a matched baseline at every demand level.
This isolates whether the corridor-champion's benefit holds away from the
nominal operating point.

All experiments share the same network geometry and demand profile; each cell is
a distinct random-seed replication.

