\textbf{Key findings (Kelvin Grove corridor, six-method ladder, five-seed means -- statistical limits in Discussion):}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{The adaptive base captures most of the attainable gain.} The DSW
adaptive plan (M1), which runs \emph{no} bus-specific decider, is the strongest
single lever: it cuts total passenger delay $4.9\%$, with buses $13\%$ faster
(10.9 vs.\ 12.5\,s/pax) and cars $10\%$ faster (29.8 vs.\ 33.2\,s/pax) -- the rare
change that improves both classes at once -- while raising throughput $5.2\%$ and
the objective $10.7\%$. The reference point for a bus-priority layer is therefore
a corridor that is already adapting its plan to demand, not a static fixed-time
plan.
\item \textbf{A per-bus priority layer rarely improves on the base, and only the
cooperative game matches it.} Layered over M1, the cooperative game (M5) ties the
base ($-4.0\%$ delay, $+9.6\%$ objective); the learned-priority arms land between
the base and neutral (MARL M2 $-1.4\%$; guarded CellQLearn M3 $+0.2\%$); and the
bus-first game (M4) actively harms ($+7.9\%$ delay, the only arm to raise car
delay, to 34.3\,s/pax). On a corridor the adaptive base already times well, the
seconds a bus grant borrows are hard to repay, so the priority layers that win are
the ones that mostly \emph{defer} to the base.
\item \textbf{The gains are throughput-positive, not a starvation artefact.}
Every adaptive method pushes $3.7$--$5.2\%$ more passenger-equivalent passages
through the corridor than fixed-time (\cref{tab:kg_results}), so the delay
reductions reflect genuinely faster service rather than fewer served passengers --
the demand-starvation check passes for all six rungs.
\item \textbf{The failure mode is instability, and here it scales with
intervention.} M4 is the only heavy actor ($\sim$9 commits/run, \cref{tab:kg_tsp})
and the only loser. The guarded CellQLearn (M3) improves on four of five seeds
($-3$ to $-8\%$) but a single demand realisation (seed 500) blows up $+24\%$ and
drags its mean to neutral: the per-action predicted benefit remains essentially
uncorrelated with the realised network outcome. The net-benefit test and
projected-saturation cascade (\cref{sec:gate_cascade}) shrink this tail but do not
remove it -- grounding the reward in measured delay and guarding the corridor's
unmonitored terminal junctions are the levers that target the residual
(see Discussion).
\item \textbf{Offset recoverability separates a helpful corridor from a
harmful one.} A cycle-lengthening grant only preserves the coordinated offset
if the seconds it borrows can be trimmed back the same cycle
(\cref{eq:online_recover}, headroom $\hat{R}_{i,t}$ of
\cref{eq:recoverable}). On Kelvin Grove the cross phases carry slack, so
extensions recover and TSP nets a gain; on the saturated Logan Road corridor the
cross phases sit at their minimum green ($\hat{R}_{i,t}\!\to\!0$), so an ungated
extension cannot be repaid, the offset drifts, and the green wave breaks -- the
lose--lose outcome where both bus \emph{and} car delay rise. Enforcing the
recoverability constraint (clamp to $\hat{R}_{i,t}$, else stand down; prefer the
cycle-neutral GR/OC actions) makes the acting arms hold the coordinated plan
rather than degrade it, which is why the champion's correct behaviour on Logan is
to stand down to $\approx$NO\_TSP rather than to intervene.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Demand sensitivity (Phase 3).}  Sweeping the corridor champion across six
car-demand scalars (0.6--1.6) shows the benefit is concentrated near the nominal
operating point: at scalar~1.0 the champion reduces delay, but at both lower and
higher demand its most-frequent-intervention setting is roughly neutral and its
default-frequency setting is mildly harmful. The corridor-level gain therefore
does \emph{not} robustly generalise across the full demand range -- a limitation
we attribute to the residual mismatch between the priced (local\,+\,one-route
cascade) cost and the true network response under heavy loading.

