The admission rule decides, but the MILP defines what the rule compares.
Dropping the physical model (queues, clocks, offsets) leaves the rule with
nothing to price; dropping R1 leaves an accurate price unused. The minimal
deployable subset supported by this evaluation is therefore: (i) a bus-benefit
estimator with schedule conditioning, (ii) a measured cross-cost plus a
downstream-cascade term, and (iii) a hard net-benefit test with
phase/cooldown/spillback feasibility---R1+R4 plus clocks and queues. Phase
rotation and variable-phase skipping are not necessary on Kelvin Grove; the
bus-first game layer (M4) is retained as a benchmark and is the only rung that
underperforms the fixed-time baseline, whereas its cooperative counterpart (M5)
is among the best.

\paragraph{Statistical limits and calibration.}
All headline numbers are means over five eval seeds (300--700); no confidence
intervals, standard deviations, or significance tests are reported, so no
claim of statistical significance is made--``best'' throughout means best
five-seed mean, and the demand-sensitivity sweep (Phase~3) is the only
robustness check beyond those seeds. Calibration was held fixed and fair
across arms: identical state feed, identical guard protocol A0, identical eval
seeds, per-arm veto/gain parameters disclosed in \cref{tab:methods}.
The one underperforming arm (M4, $+7.9\%$) received the same calibration budget;
its failure is the finding, not a setup artefact--\cref{tab:kg_tsp} shows it is
the only arm that intervenes heavily, committing where the downstream cost is high,
which is exactly what a missing cascade term predicts.

\section{Conclusions and Recommendations}
\label{sec:conclusions}

This paper presented a proof-of-concept clairvoyant MILP formulation for corridor-level TSP (never solved) and a family of online heuristics evaluated over five random-seed replications on the 12-intersection Kelvin Grove Corridor only:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{The adaptive plan base carries the corridor; the best bus-priority
layer only matches it.} On the Kelvin Grove Corridor the DSW adaptive base (M1)
gives the largest net improvement ($-4.9\%$ total, $-13\%$ bus, $-10\%$ car,
$+5.2\%$ throughput, five-seed mean; no significance test claimed), and of the
four priority layers stacked on it only the cooperative game (M5) matches it
($-4.0\%$ total). Both reach the lowest bus delays ($\approx$10.8\,s/pax) by
deferring to the coordinated plan rather than overriding it.
\item \textbf{The network cost model, not the search strategy, is decisive.} On
the identical base, the bus-first game (M4) gridlocks the corridor ($+7.9\%$) and
the guarded CellQLearn (M3) blows up on one seed ($+24\%$, dragging its mean to
neutral), because a decision priced on the local cross approach with an analytic
cost cannot see the downstream gridlock its released platoon causes. The
net-benefit test and projected-saturation cascade (\cref{sec:gate_cascade}) shrink
this tail; grounding the reward in measured delay removes the residual.
\item \textbf{Transit priority wins by restraint, not by maximising bus green.}
The arms that improve total passenger delay are the ones that commit
$\lesssim$1 action per run and hold the coordinated plan; the only heavy actor is
also the only arm to raise total delay. Chasing bus delay directly (M4) loads the
cross street enough to go net-negative.
\item \textbf{The benefit is demand-sensitive.} A six-scalar demand sweep of the
champion shows the gain is concentrated near the nominal operating point and does
not robustly generalise to lighter or heavier demand -- the residual mismatch
between the priced cost and the true network response remains an open problem.
\item \textbf{The exact MILP provides a normative formulation, not a solved benchmark} (Sec.~\ref{sec:milp_status}, Theorem~\ref{thm:milp_bellman}) and a common
framework for comparing statistical solution methods across network geometries
and demand levels.
\end{enumerate}

Future work: extending the downstream cascade beyond a single route and pricing
multi-approach spillback so the benefit generalises across demand; completing the
Logan Road corridor evaluation (\cref{sec:logan}); and joint $Z_1$/$Z_4$
optimisation.

\end{linenumbers}
\end{doublespacing}

