\section{Appendix: Solution Methods}
\label{sec:solutions}
% ==============================================================================

The exact program of \cref{sec:milp} is a whole-horizon,
clairvoyant, integer-time traffic model.  The implementation does not solve it jointly:
at each decision epoch $t \in \Tsp^{\text{trig}}$--a bus detection, a timer
tick under \texttt{CONTINUOUS\_MONITOR\_MODE}, or an empty-phase event--a single
junction solves a single-action greedy subproblem from an \emph{estimated}
state $\hat{X}_t$ (measured flows/queues, Kalman ETA when a bus is present, live phase, remaining
time, POG, pressures), commits at integer-second resolution, and advances.  This section
therefore defines (i) the online guard set shared by every rung,
(ii) the shared selection layer, and
(iii) the priority layers added on top of the plan (\cref{tab:methods} maps
the five methods).  Online
guards, the POG tracker, the bus-detection triggers, and the conditional-priority
rule layer R1--R11 live only here. They never constrain the exact program: the
program of \cref{sec:milp} contains only what a signal setting can change,
and every mechanism that \emph{observes} the corridor --- detection, cooldown,
spillback, congestion, veto, Purdue coordination, and the rule layer
(Sec.~\ref{sec:rule_tsp}) --- is part of the solution algorithm, not of the
program it solves. The two therefore differ in kind, not in degree: the program
has no notion of \emph{right now}.
All online quantities are integers (seconds, steps, vehicles, pax\,s).
\cref{tab:method_map} maps every rung to its propose/commit branch,
and \cref{tab:state_space} with Fig.~\ref{fig:state_space} detail the estimated
state those branches observe; \cref{tab:methods} then gives the scored-run
parameters for the same methods.

\subsection{Relation to the exact program}
\label{sec:milp_online_map}

The exact program decides the signal plan; the online methods of this appendix decide \emph{action letters} that perturb a warm-start
schedule one event at a time.  The two are connected by the phase perturbation
each letter induces, which is the same mechanism the program prices through
the queue recursion:
\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Letter & Phase perturbation (relative to warm start $\bar{x}$) & Cycle effect \\
\hline
\texttt{GE} & extends the bus-phase green by $\delta$ steps & $+\delta$ \\
\texttt{INS} & inserts a $\delta$-step bus phase & $+\delta$ \\
\texttt{ER} & truncates the bus-phase green by $\delta$ steps & $-\delta$ \\
\texttt{GR} & moves $\delta$ steps from a donor phase to the bus phase & $0$ \\
\texttt{VP} & skips intermediate phases (zero green on them) & $-\lvert\text{skipped}\rvert$ \\
\texttt{PT} & reorders the phase sequence & $0$ \\
\texttt{OC} & shifts the whole plan by $\Delta$ steps & $0$ net \\
\texttt{NA} & the warm start itself & $0$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Because the program's decision is the signal plan, none of these letters is a
variable of \cref{sec:milp}; the online controller chooses a letter, applies
its phase perturbation to the warm start, and commits the resulting schedule
for the integer duration it selects.  This is the sense in which the action
space of \cref{sec:actions} is the \emph{candidate generator} for the
program's decision, and the program is the yardstick it is measured against.

