\paragraph{Purdue coordination (arrivals-on-green) state and progression-loss phase (exact).}
The deployed controller tracks a live arrivals-on-green fraction per junction
(\texttt{\_pog\_ewma}, \texttt{\_sample\_progression}) and vetoes an
offset-shifting grant whose displaced main-platoon pax\,s
(\texttt{\_progression\_cost}) exceeds \texttt{PROGRESSION\_MAX\_LOSS\_PAXS};
the learner variant additionally pays that loss inside its reward
(\texttt{BXT\_POG\_REWARD} $\times$ \texttt{BXT\_POG\_WEIGHT}).  The exact
counterpart is three integer variables per $(i,t)$--POG state
$\pi^{\text{POG}}_{i,t} \in \{0,\dots,100\}$, shift magnitude
$o^{\text{shift}}_{i,t}$, and displaced loss $L^{\text{prog}}_{i,t}$--held by
$(X_t,u_t)$ exactly as the auxiliary-variable lemma
(Sec.~\ref{sec:holding}) requires.  Let
$\alpha^{\text{POG}} \in \{0,\dots,100\}$ be the integer EWMA weight
($8$ in the runs) and $\mathbf{1}^{\text{main}}_{i,t} = x_{i,p_b^i,t}$ the
main-green indicator (1 iff the coordinated phase is live, else 0):
\begin{align}
  100\,\pi^{\text{POG}}_{i,t} &= (100 - \alpha^{\text{POG}})\,\pi^{\text{POG}}_{i,t-1} + \alpha^{\text{POG}}\cdot 100 \cdot \mathbf{1}^{\text{main}}_{i,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \setminus \{0\} \label{eq:pog_ewma} \\[3pt]
  \pi^{\text{POG}}_{i,0} &= 100 && \forall i \in \Isp \label{eq:pog_init} \\[3pt]
  o^{\text{shift}}_{i,t} &= s^{\text{in}}_{i,t} - s^{\text{out}}_{i,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:pog_shift} \\[3pt]
  L^{\text{prog}}_{i,t} &\ge \pi^{\text{POG}}_{i,t}\, q^{\text{main}}_{i,t}\, o^{\text{shift}}_{i,t}\, \rho_{\text{car}} - M^{\text{big}}\,(1 - \!\!\sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} y_{i,k,t})
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:pog_loss} \\[3pt]
  L^{\text{prog}}_{i,t} &\le M^{\text{big}}\!\sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} y_{i,k,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:pog_loss_zero}
\end{align}
\cref{eq:pog_ewma,eq:pog_init} is the integer ($\times 100$)
EWMA of the code (protect-while-coordinated: loss bites only where POG is
high); \cref{eq:pog_shift} meters the clock displacement of each letter
through the signed shift $o^{\text{shift}} = s^{\text{in}} - s^{\text{out}}$
(GE/PI push the clock later, ER pulls it earlier and so displaces the platoon
in the opposite direction, GR/OC/NA contribute $0$, matching
\cref{eq:pog_loss,eq:pog_loss_zero})
price the displaced platoon as POG $\times$ main flow $\times$ shift
($\times$ occupancy into pax\,s; the code's $s^2$ is linearised here to
$s$ per step and summed over the held block, keeping the program linear-exact
per step).  \cref{eq:pog_loss} is written in compact bilinear form for
readability; it is MILP-linear as implemented because $o^{\text{shift}}_{i,t}$
is a signed sum of binaries ($\pm(y_{\text{GE}}+y_{\text{PI}}+y_{\text{ER}})$),
so $\pi^{\text{POG}}_{i,t}\,o^{\text{shift}}_{i,t}$ is a signed sum of
binary$\times$bounded-integer ($-1$--$2$) products, each linearised exactly by
its McCormick envelope (a negative shift is clamped to zero loss by the lower
bound, which is the correct reading: ER pulls the platoon earlier, never off
the back of its own green).  The loss enters the online program only, mirroring the code: it is scored
inside the methods' action ranking and it is the hard progression veto of R8
(\cref{eq:rule_prog}).  It is \emph{not} an exact-objective term
(Sec.~\ref{sec:objectives}), which keeps $Z_1$ an exactly-computed single
currency.  All quantities are integers (POG $0$--$100$,
flows $\times 100$, shifts in steps), so no fractional state enters $X_t$.


\subsubsection{Bus-Optional (Trigger-Agnostic) Operation: the Program Does Not Need Buses}
\label{sec:bus_optional}
The clauses above are stated bus-conditionally so the same program runs with
or without buses--the deployed continuous-monitor mode
(\texttt{CONTINUOUS\_MONITOR\_MODE}) evaluates
the signal-action set on the current traffic state every cadence even when no
bus is present (car-only objective), and Rule~C truncates an empty phase
toward the most-pressured phase with no bus at all.  Formally, let
$\Tsp^{\text{trig}} \subseteq \Tsp$ be the integer decision epochs (timer
ticks every $T^{\text{mon}}$ steps plus every bus-detection step), with
epoch indicator $e^{\text{trig}}_{i,t} = 1 \iff t \in \Tsp^{\text{trig}}$, and
let $b^{\text{present}}_{i,t} \in \{0,1\}$ flag bus support at $(i,t)$:
\begin{align}
  b^{\text{present}}_{i,t} &\le \sum_{b \in \Bsp}\; \sum_{\lambda:\,|\lambda - t| \le H^{\text{det}}} e_{b,i,\lambda}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:bus_present} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} s_{i,k,t} &\le e^{\text{trig}}_{i,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:trig_epoch} \\[3pt]
  e^{\text{trig}}_{i,t} &\le b^{\text{present}}_{i,t} + \mathbf{1}[t \bmod T^{\text{mon}} = 0] + \sum_{p \in \Psp_i} o^{\text{empty}}_{i,p,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:trig_source}
\end{align}
so a start needs an epoch, and an epoch needs at least one of: a bus within
$H^{\text{det}}$, a timer tick, or an empty-phase trigger.  When
$\Bsp = \emptyset$ (or no bus is within the horizon) the bus clock block
(\cref{eq:arr_assign,eq:arr_init}), the schedule cap
(\cref{eq:sched_cap}), $Z_3$, and the bus terms of $Z_1$ are vacuous--they
quantify over an empty set--and the program reduces to the car-only plus
Purdue objective $\sum_{i,t}(w_{\text{main}}\rho_{\text{car}}D_{i,\text{main},\text{car},t} + w_{\text{side}}\rho_{\text{car}}D_{i,\text{side},\text{car},t} + w^{\text{POG}}L^{\text{prog}}_{i,t})$
subject to conditions, queues, offsets, progression, Purdue, and timing rules.
A no-bus grant is then admissible only if its car-only net gain clears
$G^{\text{mon}}_{\min}$ (\cref{eq:monitor_gain}); the all--no-action
schedule remains feasible, so the bus-free program degrades gracefully to
fixed time exactly as the bus-conditional one does.

\noindent\textbf{The balance guard (R1).}  A pre-action cost-benefit check that
blocks TSP when the car cost outweighs the bus benefit by more than a threshold
ratio.  The guard compares bus passenger-seconds saved against car
passenger-seconds lost: if $\Delta_{\text{car}} > \Delta_{\text{bus}} \cdot
\theta$, the action is blocked.  It therefore admits only interventions whose
bus passenger-second savings outweigh the car passenger-second cost by the
calibrated ratio $\theta$, and it is the same trade-off written as an
integer-linear constraint in \cref{eq:rule_balance}.  WaveGate and NashGate
implement it explicitly; CPD-QL and
CellQLearn encode the same trade-off softly in their reward penalties
instead of a hard pre-commit veto.  Like every other guard here, it is applied
to a candidate \emph{at the tick it is proposed} and is not a constraint of the
exact program of \cref{sec:milp}.


\subsubsection{TSP Philosophy: Traditional Rules as Policy Constraints}
\label{sec:rule_tsp}

Field TSP practice grants priority conditionally, never unconditionally: help
a bus only when it is needed and when the corridor can afford it.  This
subsection writes that philosophy as explicit integer-linear policy
constraints R1--R9 over the exact variables.  Let
$\Ucal(X_t)$ be physical feasibility
(\cref{eq:gate_excl,eq:gate_dwell},
\ref{eq:g_bounds}--\ref{eq:c_bounds}) and
$\Ucal_{\text{rule}}(X_t) = \Ucal(X_t) \cap \{\text{R1--R9}\}$ the
rule-compliant set.  The physics-only optimum benchmarks what the street allows;
the rule-compliant optimum benchmarks what a disciplined operator should do.
Local policy parameters below are operator-chosen integers (steps, veh,
pax\,s): horizon $H^{\text{det}}$, cooldown $\kappa_{\text{cool}}$, storage
$Q^{\text{jam}}_{j}$, takeover lateness $\sigma^{\text{takeover}}$,
warrant $\gamma$, Purdue cap $P^{\text{max}}$, timing conditions
$T^{\text{minActive}}/H^{\text{hold}}/T^{\text{mon}}/G^{\text{mon}}_{\min}$,
and empty thresholds $Q^{\text{empty}}/Q^{\text{press}}$.  \cref{sec:online_guards} implements these same
rules on the estimated state $\hat{X}_t$ (measured queues, live phases,
veto ratios, congestion fractions); Appendix~A0 executes them per event.

\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{p{3.2cm}p{5.2cm}p{6.2cm}}
\hline
Traditional rule & Philosophy & Exact policy constraint \\
\hline
R1 Conditional priority / balance & Grant only if bus pax\,s saved exceed car pax\,s cost by a warrant & \cref{eq:rule_balance}: $y$-active $\Rightarrow$ net benefit $\ge \gamma$ \\
R2 Wrong-phase rejection & GE extends a live bus green; PI inserts from red &
\cref{eq:rule_phase_ge,eq:rule_phase_pi}: starts limited by baseline $\bar{x}_{i,p,t}$ \\
R3 Dwell / cooldown & Minimum spacing between grants; no oscillation & \cref{eq:rule_cool}: at most one grant per $\kappa_{\text{cool}}$ window \\
R4 Spillback protection & Never release a platoon into a blocked downstream link & \cref{eq:rule_spill_jam,eq:rule_spill_block}: jam indicator blocks GE/PI \\
R5 Detection horizon & No grant on unreliable far-future ETAs; timer/empty epochs need no bus & \cref{eq:rule_horizon}: starts within $H^{\text{det}}$ of $\tau_{b,i}$ \emph{or} on a bus-free epoch $e^{\text{trig}}_{i,t} = 1$ \\
R6 Schedule takeover & Critically late buses override R3/R5 spacing & \cref{eq:rule_take}: lateness indicator lifts R3/R5 \\
R7 Capacity legality & Min/max green and cycle bounds always hold & \cref{eq:g_bounds,eq:c_bounds} (physics = rule here) \\
R8 Purdue progression preservation & Never push a coordinated platoon off green & \cref{eq:rule_prog}: offset-shifting grant vetoed when POG-weighted loss $> P^{\text{max}}$ \\
R9 When-to-apply timing (phase-active / hold / empty-skip / monitor) & Act only when the controller timing allows it & \cref{eq:rule_minactive,eq:monitor_gain}: phase-active dwell, NA hold, empty truncation, car-only gain \\
R10 Opposing-direction arbitration & One bus-phase window per cycle; never serve both directions into it & \cref{eq:rule_arbitrate}: same-window exclusivity, schedule-lateness tie-break \\
R11 Multi-bus headway coupling & Never strand the following bus of a platoon & \cref{eq:rule_headway}: the hold covers the convoy, not the lead bus \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\paragraph{R1 Conditional priority (balance).} Let $\Psi_{i,t}$ (integer
pax\,s) be the predicted bus saving and $\Lambda_{i,t}$ the predicted car
cost at $(i,t)$ ($\Psi$/$\Lambda$ are local auxiliaries used only here).
They are fixed from the corridor state $X_t$ by the same queue-waiting sums
as $Z_1$: with $D^{\text{NA}}_{i,s,v,t}$ the no-action delay obtained from
$X_t$ under $y_{i,\text{NA},t}=1$ and $D_{i,s,v,t}$ the delay under the
chosen $u_t$ (both via \cref{eq:mv_q,eq:mv_waitsum}),
\begin{equation}
  \Psi_{i,t} \le \rho_{\text{bus}}\!\sum_{t}\!(D^{\text{NA}}_{i,\text{main},\text{bus},t}-D_{i,\text{main},\text{bus},t}),\quad
  \Lambda_{i,t} \ge \rho_{\text{car}}\!\sum_{t}\!(D_{i,\text{side},\text{car},t}-D^{\text{NA}}_{i,\text{side},\text{car},t})
  \label{eq:rule_balance_def}
\end{equation}
so $(\Psi_{i,t},\Lambda_{i,t}) = g(X_t,u_t)$ is a state-feedback output, not
a free input.  With integer warrant
$\gamma \ge 0$ (2--15\,s$\times$pax in the scored runs; $\gamma$ appears only
here and in \cref{eq:rule_balance_def}):
\begin{equation}
  \Psi_{i,t} - \Lambda_{i,t} \;\ge\; \gamma - M^{\text{big}}\Bigl(1 - \sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} y_{i,k,t}\Bigr)
  \qquad \forall i \in \Isp,\, t \in \Tsp
  \label{eq:rule_balance}
\end{equation}
so any granted step must be strictly net-beneficial; with $\gamma = 0$ this is
the classical ``is the bus late and does it pay?'' test, and
\texttt{NA} ($\sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} y = 0$) is always feasible.  The online
cost veto ($\text{cpc} > \theta \cdot \text{bps}$) and net-benefit test are
the estimated-state versions of this constraint.

\paragraph{R2 Wrong-phase rejection.} Starts (not continuations) are limited by
the integer baseline operation $\bar{x}_{i,p,t}$:
\begin{align}
  s_{i,\text{GE},t} &\le \bar{x}_{i,p_b^i,t} && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:rule_phase_ge} \\[3pt]
  s_{i,\text{PI},t} &\le 1 - \bar{x}_{i,p_b^i,t} && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:rule_phase_pi}
\end{align}
GE may only start while the bus phase is live; PI may only start from red.
Continuation steps need no sensor: coupling
the phase coupling already forces $x_{i,p_b^i,t} = 1$
while $y$ persists.

\paragraph{R3 Cooldown.} With integer spacing $\kappa_{\text{cool}} \ge 1$
($\kappa$ appears only here and in Sec.~\ref{sec:online_guards}; one cycle
plus $\max(30, 0.3\,C_i)$ steps in the runs; $t'$ is a bound dummy):
\begin{equation}
  \sum_{t'=t}^{\min(t+\kappa_{\text{cool}}-1,T)} \sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} y_{i,k,t'} \;\le\; 1
  \qquad \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp
  \label{eq:rule_cool}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{R4 Spillback protection.} For each directed link $(i,j) \in \Esp$,
let $\zeta_{ij,t} \in \{0,1\}$ indicate predicted downstream jam at $(j,t)$
($\zeta$ appears only here and in \cref{eq:rule_spill_block}).  It is a
same-step state-feedback flag on the downstream corridor-state entries
$Q_{j,p,t} \in X_t$ for integer $j$ at the same integer $t$ as the upstream
decision $y_{i,\cdot,t}$: with aggregation
\begin{equation}
  Q_{j,t} = \sum_{g \in \Gsp_j} G_{j,g,t}
    = \sum_{m \in \Msp_j} Q_{j,m,t}
    \qquad \forall j \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp
  \label{eq:rule_spill_agg}
\end{equation}
the flag is forced by the integer queue against integer storage
$Q^{\text{jam}}_{j}$:
\begin{align}
  Q_{j,t} - Q^{\text{jam}}_{j} + 1 &\le M^{\text{big}}\, \zeta_{ij,t}
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:rule_spill_jam} \\[3pt]
  Q^{\text{jam}}_{j} - Q_{j,t} &\le M^{\text{big}}\,(1-\zeta_{ij,t})
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:rule_spill_zero} \\[3pt]
  y_{i,\text{GE},t} &\le 1 - \zeta_{ij,t}
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:rule_spill_block_ge} \\[3pt]
  y_{i,\text{PI},t} &\le 1 - \zeta_{ij,t}
    && \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:rule_spill_block}
\end{align}
Any action that opens a phase serving the jammed link $(i,j)$ releases a fresh
platoon into it: GE and PI force the bus phase green and GR moves donor-green
steps into it (the phase coupling), so all three are
blocked; ER shortens the bus green and NA holds the baseline, so both always
pass.  Compactly, with
$\Ksp^{\text{rel}}_{i,(i,j)} = \{\text{GE}, \text{PI}, \text{GR}\}$ when
$p_b^i \in \Psp_{i\to j}$ and $\emptyset$ otherwise:
\begin{equation}
  \sum_{k \in \Ksp^{\text{rel}}_{i,(i,j)}} y_{i,k,t} \;\le\; 1 - \zeta_{ij,t}
  \qquad \forall (i,j) \in \Esp,\; t \in \Tsp
  \label{eq:rule_spill_block_all}
\end{equation}
\cref{eq:rule_spill_block_all} subsumes
\cref{eq:rule_spill_block_ge,eq:rule_spill_block}.  Since the
online action exclusivity holds at most one letter at
$1$, under a downstream jam the action index is forced into
$\{\text{NA}, \text{ER}\}$ plus a GR on a junction whose bus phase does
\emph{not} serve the jammed link -- hold, truncate, or reallocate a phase that
does not point into the jam, never release a fresh platoon into it.  The
online extensions VP and PT are governed by the same flag: skipping to, or
rotating to, a phase whose movements discharge into a jammed $(i,j)$ is
forbidden (Sec.~\ref{sec:online_guards} applies the identical
$\hat{\nu}_{i,t}^{\text{spill}}$ test to the phase selected).  The physical
counterpart is
\cref{eq:storage_cap,eq:discharge_space}: the rule trips at
$Q^{\text{jam}}$ before the street fills at $K$, and discharge metering makes
the jam physically binding, not just policy.  Online the same channel is read
from the measured-state spillback flag
$\hat{\nu}_{i,t}^{\text{spill}}$ (\cref{eq:online_spill_def},
Sec.~\ref{sec:online_guards}); the projected-saturation cascade of
Sec.~\ref{sec:gate_cascade} prices it one step further by propagating the
released platoon into $\hat{Q}_{j,t+}$ before thresholding.

\paragraph{R5 Detection horizon (bus-optional).} With integer reliability radius
$H^{\text{det}}$ ($H$ appears only here and in Sec.~\ref{sec:bus_optional}; 120\,s window in the runs), a bus-supported start
must lie within $H$ steps of some bus arrival at $i$, reusing the assignment
$e_{b,i,\lambda}$ ($\lambda$ is a bound dummy)--but a bus-free monitor or
empty-phase epoch needs no bus at all (Sec.~\ref{sec:bus_optional}):
\begin{equation}
  \sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} s_{i,k,t} \;\le\; \sum_{b \in \Bsp}\; \sum_{\lambda:\,|\lambda - t| \le H^{\text{det}}} e_{b,i,\lambda} + e^{\text{trig}}_{i,t}\,(1 - b^{\text{present}}_{i,t})
  \qquad \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp
  \label{eq:rule_horizon}
\end{equation}
Far-future grants with no supporting arrival \emph{and} no trigger epoch are infeasible under the rules
(but may be model-feasible).  Since $e_{b,i,\lambda}=1 \iff
\tau_{b,i}=\lambda$ (\cref{eq:arr_assign,eq:arr_link}), the
bus-supported part of the right-hand side is again a function of the bus-clock entries of $X_t$:
the start $s_{i,k,t}$ at $(i,t)$ reads the corridor bus-arrival state at the
same $t$ within radius $H^{\text{det}}$ when a bus is present, and reads only
$e^{\text{trig}}_{i,t}$ when it is not (then \cref{eq:trig_source} forces the
epoch to be a timer tick or an empty-phase trigger).

\paragraph{R6 Schedule takeover.} Let $\chi_{b,i} \in \{0,1\}$ flag a critically
late bus against integer threshold $\sigma^{\text{takeover}}$ ($\chi$ and
$\sigma^{\text{takeover}}$ appear only here and in
\cref{eq:rule_cool_take,eq:rule_horizon_take} below).  Since
$\sigma_{b,i}^+ \in X_t$, $\chi_{b,i}$ is again a state-feedback output,
held exactly (both directions, integer-tight):
\begin{align}
  \sigma_{b,i}^+ - \sigma^{\text{takeover}} + 1 \;\le\; M^{\text{big}}\, \chi_{b,i},
  \qquad
  \sigma^{\text{takeover}} - \sigma_{b,i}^+ \;\le\; M^{\text{big}}\,(1-\chi_{b,i})
  \qquad \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b
  \label{eq:rule_take} \\[3pt]
  \chi_{b,i} \le \sigma_{b,i}^+ \qquad \forall b \in \Bsp,\; i \in \Rcal_b
  \label{eq:rule_take_zero}
\end{align}
Takeover lifts spacing and horizon by augmenting their right-hand sides
explicitly (preventing cascading timetable failure dominates all other policy
terms; capacity \cref{eq:g_bounds,eq:c_bounds} is never lifted):
\begin{align}
  \sum_{t'=t}^{\min(t+\kappa_{\text{cool}}-1,T)} \sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} y_{i,k,t'}
  &\le 1 + M^{\text{big}}\!\sum_{b:\,i \in \Rcal_b}\!\chi_{b,i}
  && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:rule_cool_take} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} s_{i,k,t}
  &\le \sum_{b \in \Bsp}\; \sum_{\lambda:\,|\lambda - t| \le H^{\text{det}}} e_{b,i,\lambda}
     + e^{\text{trig}}_{i,t}\,(1 - b^{\text{present}}_{i,t})
     + M^{\text{big}}\!\sum_{b:\,i \in \Rcal_b}\!\chi_{b,i}
  && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:rule_horizon_take}
\end{align}
\cref{eq:rule_cool_take,eq:rule_horizon_take} replace
Eqs.~\ref{eq:rule_cool} and \ref{eq:rule_horizon} whenever takeover is
modelled; with all $\chi_{b,i}=0$ they collapse exactly to the base
R3/R5 forms, so the flag is connected both ways (sets the bound, is set by
$X_t$).

\paragraph{R8 Purdue progression preservation (controller dvar).} An
offset-shifting grant ($k \in \Ksp^{\text{len}}$) is admissible only if the
POG-weighted platoon it displaces does not exceed the integer cap
$P^{\text{max}}$ (\texttt{PROGRESSION\_MAX\_LOSS\_PAXS}):
\begin{equation}
  L^{\text{prog}}_{i,t} \;\le\; P^{\text{max}} + M^{\text{big}}\Bigl(1 - \sum_{k \in \Ksp^{\text{len}}} y_{i,k,t}\Bigr)
  \qquad \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp
  \label{eq:rule_prog}
\end{equation}
with $L^{\text{prog}}_{i,t}$ held by \cref{eq:pog_ewma,eq:pog_loss_zero}.
GR/OC/NA carry no shift ($o^{\text{shift}}_{i,t} = 0$) and pass trivially;
GE/PI/VP/PT must price their shift.  Online the loss is priced in the action
score (\texttt{BXT\_POG\_REWARD}), mirroring the code where the veto blocks and the reward teaches, while
\texttt{MONITOR\_PROG\_WEIGHT} subtracts the same POG-weighted loss inside the
car-only monitor reward before the $G^{\text{mon}}_{\min}$ warrant test:
veto first, learn second, price always.

\paragraph{R9 When-to-apply timing: phase-active dwell, no-action hold, empty-skip, monitor gain.}
The code refuses to re-action a just-started green (\texttt{MIN\_PHASE\_ACTIVE\_S}),
debounces NA re-evaluation (\texttt{NO\_ACTION\_HOLD\_S}), truncates an empty
phase toward pressure (Rule~C, \texttt{EMPTY\_PHASE\_SKIP\_MODE}), and acts on
the traffic state with no bus when the car-only gain clears
\texttt{CONTINUOUS\_MONITOR\_MIN\_GAIN\_PAXS}.  Exactly, with integer
$T^{\text{minActive}}$, $H^{\text{hold}}$, $T^{\text{mon}}$, $G^{\text{mon}}_{\min}$,
$Q^{\text{empty}}$, $Q^{\text{press}}$ from Sec.~\ref{sec:milp}:
\begin{align}
  \sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} s_{i,k,t} &\le 1 - \sum_{p \in \Psp_i}\; \sum_{t' = \max(0,\,t-T^{\text{minActive}}+1)}^{t-1} w^{\text{on}}_{i,p,t'}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:rule_minactive} \\[3pt]
  \sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} s_{i,k,t} &\le 1 - h_{i,t}, \qquad
  h_{i,t} \ge y_{i,\text{NA},\tau} - \!\!\sum_{k \ne \text{NA}}\sum_{t' = \tau+1}^{t} y_{i,k,t'}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp,\; \tau \in [\max(0,t-H^{\text{hold}}+1),\,t] \label{eq:rule_hold} \\[3pt]
  w^{\text{off}}_{i,p,t} &\ge o^{\text{empty}}_{i,p,t} - \!\!\sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} y_{i,k,t}
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; p \in \Psp_i,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:rule_empty} \\[3pt]
  \Psi_{i,t} - \Lambda_{i,t} &\ge G^{\text{mon}}_{\min} - M^{\text{big}}\, b^{\text{present}}_{i,t} - M^{\text{big}}\Bigl(1 - \sum_{k \ne \text{NA}} y_{i,k,t}\Bigr)
    && \forall i \in \Isp,\; t \in \Tsp \label{eq:monitor_gain}
\end{align}
\cref{eq:rule_minactive} blocks a start until the current phase has been
active $T^{\text{minActive}}$ steps (no $w^{\text{on}}$ in the look-back;
$T^{\text{minActive}} = 0$ disables it).  \cref{eq:rule_hold} carries the
NA hold: $h_{i,t} = 1$ for $H^{\text{hold}}$ steps after a NA step unless an
intervention supervenes.  \cref{eq:rule_empty} forces the empty phase to
close (advance toward pressure) when $o^{\text{empty}}_{i,p,t} = 1$, unless a
priority grant already owns the step.  \cref{eq:monitor_gain} is the
bus-free warrant: when $b^{\text{present}}_{i,t} = 0$ a grant must clear the
car-only gain $G^{\text{mon}}_{\min}$; when a bus is present the bus-aware R1
(\cref{eq:rule_balance}) governs instead.  All four are state feedback on
$(X_t,u_t)$--phase starts, NA history, pressures, and presence--so the timing
layer is closed exactly as R1--R9 are.

\paragraph{R10 Opposing-direction window arbitration.} A junction serves one
bus phase per cycle, but the corridor carries buses in both directions; when
two buses $b, b'$ on opposing movements both need the same window $w$, only
one fits.  The window is a single-machine slot (R10 is the capacity side of
the same slot that the causal ordering of
Sec.~\ref{sec:online_guards} fills), so the program must \emph{choose}:
\begin{equation}
  w^{\text{served}}_{b,i} + w^{\text{served}}_{b',i} \;\le\; 1
  \qquad \forall i \in \Isp,\; b,b' \text{ opposing at } i
  \label{eq:rule_arbitrate}
\end{equation}
and the tie is broken by schedule-lateness-adjusted urgency: the bus with the
larger deviation $\sigma^+_{b,i}$ (R6) is served first, ties by earlier ETA,
and the loser takes the next cycle window (or an INS within reach and the
repayable budget).  This is a policy choice, not a safety one -- the intergreen
and min-green bounds (R7) still separate the two windows.  Without R10 the
program could serve both via two phase entries in one cycle, silently
halving the cross capacity the controller is supposed to protect.

\paragraph{R11 Multi-bus headway coupling.} Serving the first bus of a
platoon must not strand the second.  If $b'$ follows $b$ on the same
movement within the headway $H^{\text{platoon}}$, a grant to $b$ commits the
window to also clear $b'$ (the green-keep hold), so the perturbation budget
is the combined hold and the benefit is the combined occupancy:
\begin{equation}
  \delta^{\text{hold}}_{i} \;\ge\; \delta^{\text{clear}}_{b,i} +
    \mathbf{1}[\tau_{b',i} - \tau_{b,i} \le H^{\text{platoon}}]\;
    \delta^{\text{clear}}_{b',i}
  \qquad \forall i \in \Isp,\; b' \text{ following } b
  \label{eq:rule_headway}
\end{equation}
R11 is what makes the green wave a \emph{platoon} motion rather than a single
vehicle: the window, the offset shift it causes, and the progression loss
it prices are all computed for the convoy, not the lead bus alone.

Together R1--R11 formalise the conditional-priority principle of the classical
literature---intervene for a late-enough bus or a paying bus-free epoch, in
the correct phase, when the corridor can absorb the platoon and keep its
progression, without rapid succession, net harm, or illegal timing.  Their
estimated-state forms are exactly the guards of Sec.~\ref{sec:online_guards},
executed per event by the online controller.







